    {
      "skill_id": "de-a1-008",
      "title": "Singular and plural noun forms",
      "explanation": "German forms plurals in several ways, so learn the plural with the noun. Common patterns: -e (der Tag, die Tage), -e with an umlaut (die Hand, die Hände), -er with an umlaut where possible (das Kind, die Kinder; das Haus, die Häuser), -n or -en (die Frau, die Frauen; die Lampe, die Lampen), -s (das Auto, die Autos), and no change or only an umlaut (der Lehrer, die Lehrer; der Bruder, die Brüder). The nominative plural article is always die, and the verb takes the plural ending.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "de": "Die Kinder spielen.",
          "en": "The children are playing."
        },
        {
          "de": "Die Frauen arbeiten.",
          "en": "The women work."
        },
        {
          "de": "Die Autos sind neu.",
          "en": "The cars are new."
        }
      ],
      "references": ["GOETHE-A1"],
